{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333077" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y-2,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333076" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y+6,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(-\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{27}{16}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y+6 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-3\\cdot \\frac{27}{16}+6 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{29}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{16}+\\frac{29}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{137}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | $-\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{29}{64}$ | $\\frac{137}{64}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{29}{64}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329474" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{137}{64}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-1,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4})-1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333066" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-4y \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-4\\cdot (\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,2)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-6.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})-(-6))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{17}{12}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333056" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333083" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333083" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=1.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333091" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333091" "field