d/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333369" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333369" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{3}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ |  |\n\nAlso from the table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{3}{4})$ is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 6+8\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 42 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $3$ | $6$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{17}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{17}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333253" "field" nil "a