}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333253" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈-\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=-\\frac{8}{3}+(-8)=-\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ | $-8$ | $-\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{128}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ | $-8$ | $-\\frac{32}{3}$ | $-\\frac{128}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{128}{3})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-256) \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ | $-8$ | $-\\frac{32}{3}$ | $-\\frac{128}{3}$ | $-\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=-\\frac{8}{3}+(-\\frac{64}{3})=-24\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{8}{3}$ | $-\\frac{16}{3}$ | $-8$ | $-\\frac{32}{3}$ | $-\\frac{128}{3}$ | $-\\frac{64}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-24$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-24.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-333372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{16}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa