size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(0)≈\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3+x-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =3+0-2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(-1,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{9}{2}=\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3+1-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{31}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{31}{3})+8\\cdot 3-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{83}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{83}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+(-\\frac{83}{36}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{43}{6}$ | $-\\frac{31}{3}$ | $-\\frac{83}{36}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{13}{36}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conlude that $y(1)≈\\frac{13}{36}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "922e870e6db5c51a54c9dfb121d3a37445160bc81dabdabf8c2965dd0581ccac"} {"question" "q-329310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "q-329310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/state.json" "file_sha256" "ca064442211734c1226fa7b02b7aae8942102f8584c920afa239d9d840083fbf"} {"question" "e-21539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14,$ and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21539.json" "file_sha256" "bf25fb3ac2aefac644953b91d0df8dd9321241914555de37cb377eb681b13ddc"} {"question" "e-21539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}).$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})-2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21539.json" "file_sha256" "bf25fb3ac2aefac644953b91d0df8dd9321241914555de37cb377eb681b13ddc"} {"question" "e-21565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y\\left(-2\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21565.json" "file_sha256" "86d6f69bdbc1c825f5e7e9a2ba74469fbba1e5a606115a9eb7c17c7d0e5a1601"} {"question" "e-21565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1.$\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21565.json" "file_sha256" "86d6f69bdbc1c825f5e7e9a2ba74469fbba1e5a606115a9eb7c17c7d0e5a1601"} {"question" "e-21538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21538.json" "file_sha256" "095a17845ff5de1f68829c5027fbb516e58e63911a0d9bbbb2a2a5e5cc3e4eb9"} {"question" "e-21538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x-y \\\\  & =-0-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = 1.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{7}{8})=-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-1-(-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8})+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21538.json" "file_sha256" "095a17845ff5de1f68829c5027fbb516e58e63911a0d9bbbb2a2a5e5cc3e4eb9"} {"question" "e-21566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x - 2y + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 3\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21566.json" "file_sha256" "86311ba7319b725a4a82c9f48c01da7c4c2fae338b41c6f172eef3b92af6bdf5"} {"question" "e-21566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of $y'$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = (0,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & ={x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}+6 \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot 2+6 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x, y) = (1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-2y+6 \\\\  & =1-2\\cdot 3+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}+6 \\\\  & =2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 1-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+\\frac{11}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{47}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)\\approx\\dfrac{47}{12}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14046927/example-21566.json" "file_sha256" "86311ba7319b725a4a82c9f48c01da7c4c2fae338b41c6f172eef3b92af6bdf5"}] "usage" {"e-21566" [] "e-21538" [] "e-21565" [] "q-333066" [] "q-333090" [] "e-21539" [] "q-329310" [] "q-329474" []} "basis" 1700 "retractions" [[17592186221873 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 3y + 6, y(-1) = 2$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{2}$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{27}{16}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |"] [17592186221894 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 2y - 1, y(0) =-1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"the-two-step-adams-bashforth-moulton-method\"></a>"] [17592186221895 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 4x - 2y - 2, y(1) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"completing-a-row-of-a-table-using-the-abm-2-step-method\"></a>"] [17592186221870 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 3 + x - 2x^{2}y, y(-1) = 1$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, where the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, if the step size is $Δx = 1$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $$? |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*"] [17592186221102 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x - 2y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that the first step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, fill the missing entries in the table below, where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at each step.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186221102 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method (ABM2) with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$, and\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$.\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by\n\n$$\ny_p = y + \\Delta y_p\n$$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2x - 2y + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) - 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) + 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{1}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ |"] [17592186221105 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y\\left(-2\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac14$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |"] [17592186221105 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1$.\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1$.\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1$.\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot (-1) - (-1)) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{48} \\cdot (-12) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |"] [17592186221101 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = -x - y, \\qquad y\\left(-1\\right) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next rows of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |\n| $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{}$ |  |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186221101 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in\n\n$$\ny\\left(0\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= -x - y \\\\\n&=-0 - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point\n\n$$\n(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny'_{-1} = 1\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = \\frac{1}{4} + (-\\frac{7}{8}) =-\\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= -x_{new} - y_{p} \\\\\n&=-1 - (-\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) + 8 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}) - 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot (-\\frac{39}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 1\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac{1}{4} + \\left(-\\dfrac{13}{32}\\right) = -\\dfrac{5}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{7}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{3}{8}}$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{13}{32}}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{5}{32}}\n$$"] [17592186221106 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = x - 2y + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 2$\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n$y(1) \\approx 3$\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$\\Delta y = \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x$\n*where $y_p$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y'_{-1}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*"] [17592186221106 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}Predictor &: & Δy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx, \\\\ Corrector &: & Δy|= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y)$,\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1})$, and\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p)$.\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of $y'$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = (0,2)$ is\n$y_{-1}^{′}|= x_{-1} - 2y_{-1} + 6; = 0 - 2 \\cdot 2 + 6; = 2$.\n- The slope at the current point $(x, y) = (1,3)$ is\n$y^{′}|= x - 2y + 6; = 1 - 2 \\cdot 3 + 6; = 1$.\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy_{p} &= \\frac{1}{2}(3y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\ny_{p} &= y + Δy_{p} = 3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= x_{new} - 2y_{p} + 6 \\\\\n&= 2 - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} + 6 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12}(5 \\cdot 1 + 8 \\cdot 1 - 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 11 \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new}=2\n$$\n\nTo get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{11}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{47}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{11}{12}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac{47}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2)\\approx\\dfrac{47}{12}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186220131 17592186220132 17592186220133 17592186220134 17592186220135] "immutable_fields" [[17592186220130 :answer-field/correct] [17592186220130 :answer-field/id] [17592186220130 :answer-field/key] [17592186220130 :answer-field/type] [17592186220142 :answer-field/id] [17592186220142 :answer-field/key] [17592186220142 :answer-field/type] [17592186220143 :answer-field/id] [17592186220143 :answer-field/key] [17592186220143 :answer-field/type] [17592186220144 :answer-field/id] [17592186220144 :answer-field/key] [17592186220144 :answer-field/type] [17592186220312 :answer-field/id] [17592186220312 :answer-field/key] [17592186220312 :answer-field/type] [17592186220313 :answer-field/id] [17592186220313 :answer-field/key] [17592186220313 :answer-field/type] [17592186220314 :answer-field/id] [17592186220314 :answer-field/key] [17592186220314 :answer-field/type] [17592186220315 :answer-field/id] [17592186220315 :answer-field/key] [17592186220315 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
